# Every environment file, everywhere. A secret reaches a public repository exactly once.
**/.env*
# Except the examples, which are the point: a template of the variables a developer needs, with
# placeholder values and no secrets. The rule above is deliberately wide, so each has to be named.
!**/.env.example
!**/example.env
!**/example.local.env
**/.turbo
**/.vercel

# macOS Finder metadata — never part of the source tree.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# Build outputs.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/dist
contracts/out
contracts/cache
contracts/broadcast

# contracts/lib is tracked, as four git submodules. `.gitmodules` at this root records the exact
# commit of each; the trees themselves are never committed, which is what a submodule is for.
#
# This used to be ignored and installed with --no-git, so nothing recorded what had been vendored
# and a fresh clone could not build at all. That mattered more here than in most projects: the v4
# hook's address is mined from its creation code, which includes its whole import closure, so
# moving v4-periphery by one commit silently invalidates the mined salt and the deployed hook
# reverts HookAddressNotValid.
#
# contracts/dependencies.toml and contracts/script/check-deps.sh still exist and still have a job.
# A commit pin says which tree was intended; the fingerprint says whether the tree on disk is
# actually that one, which catches a dirty or locally patched submodule.
